# The local stand-in for the production reverse proxy (ADR-0025).
#
# ## Why this exists at all
#
# Everything the client does assumes ONE origin: the session cookie travels without
# `withCredentials`, `/api/v1` is relative, and there is no CORS anywhere in the codebase. Testing
# against `localhost:3000` talking to `localhost:8791` verifies none of that — two ports are two
# origins, and Next's dev rewrite hides the difference.
#
# More importantly, BACKEND-CONTRACT §5.2 was written from measured browser behaviour THROUGH A PROXY:
# response headers are not flushed to the browser until the first byte of body arrives, so a stream
# that writes nothing until its first event leaves `EventSource` in CONNECTING for ever — while curl
# against the origin works perfectly. That asymmetry cannot be reproduced without a proxy in the path.
#
# Deliberately NOT deployment. No TLS, no domain, no DNS, no cloud. One container, on this machine, so
# the topology can be exercised before any of that exists.
#
# ## Ports 18080/18081, and why not 8080
#
# 8080 is the first port anyone reaches for, which is exactly why it is the wrong one: it was already
# serving somebody else's project on this machine, and binding 0.0.0.0:8080 in a container SHADOWED
# their process on [::1]:8080 without failing to start. Their app began answering 502 — this proxy's
# Bad Gateway, because its own upstream was not running — and nothing said why.
#
# Check before binding. `lsof -nP -iTCP:<port> -sTCP:LISTEN` costs nothing and a silently stolen port
# costs somebody an afternoon.

{
	admin off
	auto_https off
}

:18080 {
	# The API. `flush_interval -1` disables response buffering, which is what makes SSE work through
	# a proxy at all — the Caddy equivalent of nginx's `proxy_buffering off`.
	#
	# It is set here AND the backend sends `X-Accel-Buffering: no`, because the two protect against
	# different mistakes: this file is the deployment's business, and the header is what a deployment
	# that forgot gets anyway. §5.2 asks for both.
	handle /api/v1/* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8791 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}

	# Health and readiness, on the same origin so a probe exercises the same path traffic takes.
	handle /health/* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8791
	}

	# Everything else is the app.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:3000
	}
}

# The same topology WITHOUT `flush_interval`.
#
# ## This does NOT demonstrate the buffering failure, and the comment claiming it did was worse than
# ## no comment at all
#
# Caddy sets `FlushInterval = -1` by itself as soon as it sees `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, so
# removing the directive changes nothing for a stream. Measured: 13ms to the first body byte on
# :18080 against 33ms here — both flush the prelude immediately. This port was standing in as evidence
# that the buffering failure had been reproduced, and it had reproduced nothing. A demonstration that
# cannot fail is not evidence of anything.
#
# What it does show, honestly, is that Caddy's DEFAULT is safe: a deployment on Caddy that forgets
# `flush_interval` still streams.
#
# The failure was then chased into `nginx-buffering.conf` on :18082 — a second proxy told to buffer,
# with the backend's `X-Accel-Buffering: no` genuinely deleted one hop earlier — and it did not
# reproduce there either. Read that file for the five measurements, and for what a negative result
# does and does not license anyone to claim. The short version: both defences stay, and no local gate
# claims to have verified the hazard they defend against.
:18081 {
	handle /api/v1/* {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8791
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:3000
	}
}

# A backend that never sent `X-Accel-Buffering: no`, stood up by deleting it on the way out.
#
# Only reached by `nginx-buffering.conf`, and it exists because there is no other way to simulate the
# omission. nginx's `proxy_hide_header X-Accel-Buffering` hides the header from the CLIENT while
# still acting on it itself — measured: the header was absent from the response and the stream
# flushed in 32ms anyway — so hiding it proves nothing. Deleting it one hop EARLIER means the
# buffering proxy genuinely never sees it, which is the deployment §5.2 is warning about.
:18083 {
	handle {
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8791 {
			flush_interval -1
			header_down -X-Accel-Buffering
		}
	}
}

